# =====================================================================
# Java4Shell (CVE-2026-114938) — Splunk hunts
# Trigger: 64×'A' + '=' (65 chars), 128×'A' + '=' variant on JDK 17-class runtimes.
# TLS caveat: these web-log hunts only work where the request arrived in
# cleartext (terminating proxy/LB, app logging bodies/headers) — you cannot
# see the payload inside TLS. DESTRUCTIVE: never replay trigger payloads at
# production services; several stacks (Spring Security/Tomcat, Temurin
# 17.0.21+9) die pre-auth with exit 134 + hs_err log.
# Adjust index names (web:, wineventlog:, osquery/...) to your scheme.
# =====================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# (a) Trigger-string hunt in web access logs.
#     Rex extracts the raw A{64}= / A{128}= forms from URI, POST body,
#     or Authorization header. The body often only exists if a proxy or
#     the app itself logs it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
index=web sourcetype=access_combined OR sourcetype=*:iis
| eval huntfield=coalesce(uri_query, uri, cs_uri_query, cs_uri_stem) . " " . coalesce(c_textbody, form_body, "") . " " . coalesce(cs_authorization, "authorization=" . coalesce(cs_User_Agent,""))
``` Keep the actual body/auth fields your proxy populates; many proxies only log URI.```
| rex field=huntfield "(?<j4s_trig64>A{64}=)"
| rex field=huntfield "(?<j4s_trig128>A{128}=)"
| where isnotnull(j4s_trig64) OR isnotnull(j4s_trig128)
| stats min(_time) as first_seen max(_time) as last_seen count by clientip, sourcetype, uri
``` The decoder rejects AFTER corrupting, so a 401/400 status does not mean harmless.```
``` | eval decode_status=coalesce(status, sc_status)```
| sort - count

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# (b) Double-base64 trigger constant.
#     Derivation (verified with python3):
#       t = base64.b64encode(('A'*64+'=').encode())   -> inner  constant
#       c = base64.b64encode(t)                        -> double constant
#     inner  = "QUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQT0="
#     double = "UVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVQwPQ=="
#     The inner constant is what appears when an attacker base64-encodes the
#     trigger once more before sending (e.g. inside Basic credentials where the
#     whole "user:pass" is b64-encoded, or double-encoded body params).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
index=web sourcetype=access_combined OR sourcetype=*:iis
| eval huntfield=coalesce(cs_authorization, "...") . " " . coalesce(uri, cs_uri_stem) . " " . coalesce(form_body, "")
| search huntfield="*UVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVVGQlFVRkJRVUZCUVQwPQ==*"
``` Also hunt the single-encoded constant (b64 of the trigger itself):```
``` | search huntfield="*QUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQUFBQT0=*"
| stats count by clientip, uri

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# (c) hs_err_pid fatal-error log file creation (host FIM/osquery feed).
#     JVM crash artifact = prime local Java4Shell signal; correlate the
#     file's PID with a java process and check for exit 134.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
index=osquery OR index=fim sourcetype=osquery OR sourcetype=*fim
| regex filename="^hs_err_pid\d+\.log$"
``` Windows Sysmon alternative (EventCode 11):
| index=wineventlog EventCode=11 TargetFilename="*hs_err_pid*"
```
| table _time, host, filename, path, osquery_pid
| correlate with: index=host sourcetype=process| where process exit code 134/139 for java```

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# (d) Java spawning a shell process hunt.
#     Windows: Sysmon EventCode 1 parent java.exe -> cmd/powershell/LOLBins.
#     Linux: auditd/syslog (e.g. auditbeat) child sh/bash/python/perl/nc of java.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
``` Windows variant:
index=wineventlog EventCode=1
| spath ParentImage | search ParentImage="*java*.exe"
| spath Image | search Image IN ("cmd.exe","powershell.exe","wscript.exe","cscript.exe","certutil.exe","rundll32.exe","regsvr32.exe")
| table _time host ParentImage Image CommandLine User
```
``` Linux variant:
index=linux sourcetype=*auditd OR sourcetype=syslog
| rename exe as image, ppid as parent
| search "java" as parent context OR parent_process="*java*"
| where image IN ("sh","bash","dash","python","python3","perl","nc","ncat","socat","curl","wget")
| table _time host parent_process image cmdline uid
```

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# (e) IllegalArgumentException burst per client_ip.
#     The decoder throws IAE AFTER the OOB write — a flood of these per
#     client is live exploitation / trigger probing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
index=app sourcetype=*:application OR index=app sourcetype=java:log
| regex _raw="(java\.lang\.)?IllegalArgumentException"
| regex _raw="(?i)(base64|decoder|decode)"
``` restrict to decode-path errors to cut noise from ordinary bad-input IAEs```
| timechart span=5m count by client_ip
``` alert when any client_ip exceeds N IAEs in a 5m window; tune N per app baseline```
| where [ search index=app | timechart span=5m count by client_ip | eventstats max(count) as mx ]

# ---------------------------------------------------------------
# (f) Slow-POST groom hint.
#     Calif's heap Feng Shui uses many small/paused POSTs from one client
#     (request streaming / TCP-ACK pacing) to control allocation order.
#     Hunt for low-byte POST bursts sustained over several minutes.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------
index=web sourcetype=access_combined OR sourcetype=*:iis
| where http_method IN ("POST") OR cs_method="POST"
``` keep only tiny bodies — grooming uses small partial writes```
| where bytes < 2048
| timechart span=5m count as post_count, avg(bytes) as avg_bytes by clientip
``` look for: high post_count + avg_bytes very small, sustained 3+ consecutive        ```
``` windows from the same clientip. Cross-reference IAE bursts (query e).          ```
| stats count as posts sum(bytes) as total_bytes earliest(_time) as start latest(_time) as end by clientip
| where posts > 100 AND total_bytes < 2048*posts/4
| eval duration_min=(end-start)/60
| where duration_min > 3
``` these are the same client IPs doing slow-POST streaming — correlate with (a)/(e)```
